<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nakhon Si Thammarat</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ligor</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nakhon Si Thammarat is a city on the east coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, called Ligor by Europeans. It was the centre of the trading kingdom of Tambralinga and later of a kingdom that spread Sri Lankan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a> to Sukhothai. Its great stupa, Wat Phra Mahathat, remains one of the most revered in Thailand.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nakhon-si-thammarat.jpg" alt="Nakhon Si Thammarat" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Seksan Phonsuwan · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nai_Mueang,_Mueang_Nakhon_Si_Thammarat_District,_Nakhon_Si_Thammarat_80000,_Thailand_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Nakhon Si Thammarat is a city municipality and the capital of Nakhon Si Thammarat province, about 610 km south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a> on the east coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a>. The name comes from the Sanskrit Nagara Shrī Dharmarāja, and Dharmarāja means righteous ruler, an important <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> concept. The city was originally coastal, but silting has moved the coastline away. Its long north-south shape dates to its original site on a flood-safe dune, and the modern centre lies north of the Old Town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest settlement nearby was Tha Rua, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> ceramics of the twelfth century were found. The settlement then moved to Muang Phra Wieng, associated with Tambralinga, a polity under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> sea power. An inscription at Wat Sema Muang records that by 775 the king of Srivijaya had established a foothold at Ligor and built a sanctuary to the Buddha.</p><p>The Chronicles of Nakorn Si Thammarat, composed in the seventeenth century, credit King Sri Thammasok in the thirteenth century with founding the present city, building Wat Phra Mahathat and introducing Singhalese Theravada Buddhism. An inscription at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaiya">Chaiya</a> says he ruled Tambralinga in 1231. The kingdom held authority over twelve cities from Chumphon to Pahang, and the Ramkamhaeng Stele of Sukhothai names the city in 1292. The kingdom ended in the fourteenth century, and the city was re-established by a son of the ruler of Phetchaburi, after which it came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>.</p><p>In the fifteenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> appointed governors and the city became the main seat of Siamese authority in the south. Western sources called it Ligor, and the Japanese adventurer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamada-nagamasa">Yamada Nagamasa</a> was made its governor in 1629. After the fall of Ayutthaya in 1767 the vice-governor Phra Palat Nu ruled the south until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">King Taksin</a> subdued him in 1769. The Burmese sacked the city in 1786. Under Chao Phraya Nakhon Noi (1811–1838) it kept a relative autonomy. The governorship was abolished in 1896, and the city became a provincial capital in 1932.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Wat Phra Mahathat is the most important temple of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> and holds a tooth relic of the Buddha. Its chedi is 78 m high and surrounded by 173 smaller ones; it is in Sri Lankan style but is said to stand on an earlier chedi of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> period. Of the city wall, which enclosed about one square kilometre, only the northern gate and a short stretch survive.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nakhon Si Thammarat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Siam briefly broke into rival regimes at Phitsanulok, Phimai, Sawangkhaburi and <b>Nakhon Si Thammarat</b>, until the general Taksin, who had escaped the siege, drove out the remaining Burmese garrison, founded a new capital at Thonburi and reunited the kingdom, whose successor, the Chakri dynasty's Bangkok, continues the Siamese…</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Dharanindra (r. 780–800), hailed in the Kelurak inscription of 782 as &quot;the slayer of courageous enemies&quot;, took the Shailendras' power to <b>Ligor</b> on the Malay Peninsula and rose to be Maharaja of Srivijaya, by conquest or by kinship.</blockquote><a href="/empire/medang-kingdom">Medang Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The rulers adopted the Theravada Buddhism of Sri Lanka, brought by monks via <b>Nakhon Si Thammarat</b> in the south, and built monasteries such as Wat Mahathat at the centre of Sukhothai and Wat Si Chum with its giant seated Buddha.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sukhothai-kingdom">Sukhothai Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Foreigners climbed furthest: Constantine Phaulkon became Narai’s prime minister, and Yamada Nagamasa became ruler of <b>Nakhon Si Thammarat</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ayutthaya">Social Hierarchy of the Ayutthaya Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Nakhon Si Thammarat</b> Kingdom grew out of Tambralinga in the late 13th century, as the city of Nakhon Si Thammarat, called Ligor by Europeans, became a Thai-ruled Buddhist centre.</blockquote><a href="/state/nakhon-si-thammarat-kingdom">Nakhon Si Thammarat Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Its centre lay at or near <b>Nakhon Si Thammarat</b> on the east coast of the peninsula.</blockquote><a href="/state/tambralinga">Tambralinga</a></li></ul></section>
